  "worked_solution": "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},$ and $b=-z{y}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}-z{y}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}{b}^{0}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{a}^{0}{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{3}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{0}+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{2}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{1} \\\\  & \\,+3{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{1}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{2}+1{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}^{0}{(-z{y}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}})(1)+3(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}})(-z{y}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})({z}^{2}{y}^{4})+1(1)(-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}z{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{4}}{y}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}-{z}^{3}{y}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind, in ascending powers of $z$ and descending powers of $x,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{y}^{3}}-3{x}^{4}z+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$",
